WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:06.300
Should public money help pay for election campaigns?

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.500
That is the question behind California Proposition four.

3
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.040
Campaign financing pays for things like mailers, advertising, and

4
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:14.900
campaign staff.

5
00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.760
Public financing means government money helps cover those expenses.

6
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.520
Today, California generally bans that for state and local

7
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.040
candidates.

8
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.080
Some charter cities, including Los Angeles and San Francisco,

9
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.640
already have exceptions.

10
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.200
Proposition four would let more governments offer public financing.

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.920
It does not automatically launch a program or send

12
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:39.800
money to candidates.

13
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.360
Imagine a first-time candidate planning how to reach voters.

14
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.380
She has local supporters, but printing leaflets and running

15
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:49.940
advertisements cost money.

16
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.580
A future city program might match small donations.

17
00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.420
Here is a made-up example: twenty dollars from a

18
00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.860
resident, plus forty dollars from the city, gives the

19
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:01.820
campaign sixty dollars.

20
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.180
Those numbers are illustrative.

21
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.380
Proposition four does not choose a matching rate or

22
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.340
promise this candidate any money.

23
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.420
The aim is to help candidates reach more voters.

24
00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.000
You might ask: who qualifies, and who decides?

25
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:21.020
A future program would set detailed eligibility rules and

26
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:21.540
its budget.

27
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:25.860
Candidates would have to demonstrate broad support in their

28
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.740
district and accept spending limits.

29
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.960
Public money could not pay their fines, legal defense,

30
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.020
or personal campaign loans.

31
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.160
Funds reserved for education, transportation, or public safety could

32
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:38.880
not be used.

33
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.560
But other public funds spent on campaigns still have

34
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:43.720
an opportunity cost.

35
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.680
The state estimates a few hundred thousand dollars a

36
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.400
year for guidance.

37
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.200
That is separate from future campaign programs, whose costs

38
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.060
depend on later decisions and could be significant.

39
00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.940
A voter favoring reform might say: I want candidates

40
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:04.240
with community support to compete, even without wealthy backers.

41
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.340
Supporters hope matching donations would make ordinary residents more

42
00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:10.600
important to fundraising.

43
00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.820
Voting yes gives governments that option, subject to rules.

44
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.600
It does not guarantee less donor influence.

45
00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.340
Another voter might ask: should taxpayers pay for political

46
00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.120
campaigns?

47
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.180
They may dislike funding candidates they oppose, or prefer

48
00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:27.940
other uses for public money.

49
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.840
The measure leaves future program budgets and many details

50
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:33.260
undecided.

51
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.900
Public financing also does not eliminate private political spending.

52
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.320
A no vote keeps the general ban and existing

53
00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:42.180
exceptions.

54
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:44.260
What do polls show?

55
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.840
In early September, PPIC found forty-two percent yes, fifty-three

56
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.380
percent no, and five percent unsure among likely voters.

57
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.320
A later September Berkeley poll found thirty-five percent yes,

58
00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.220
thirty-five percent no, and thirty percent undecided.

59
00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:03.680
These are different surveys, not a clean trend or

60
00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:04.820
an election forecast.

61
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.380
The choice is whether to allow future public campaign

62
00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:13.100
financing programs, with potential benefits and public costs.

63
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.140
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, sources, and

64
00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:18.440
official voter guide.

65
00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.080
Then make your own informed choice.
